course: biology
lesson: 34
title: Birds of prey and owls
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Birds of prey, also called raptors, hunt other animals with sharp eyes, hooked beaks and gripping feet with curved talons.
explain: Each one has its own trick: a kestrel hangs over a motorway verge with its head perfectly still, a peregrine drops from a cathedral tower faster than any other animal, and an osprey dives feet first into a lake.
explain: Owls are the night shift: soft fringed feathers make their flight silent, and the heart-shaped face of a barn owl is a dish that funnels sound into its ears.
explain: Owls swallow mice whole and cough up the fur and bones as a pellet, which is why a class can rebuild a whole vole skeleton with tweezers.
explain: Pictures get them wrong: an owl cannot turn its head all the way round, owls are not the cleverest birds, and a bald eagle is not bald at all.
fact: Motorway verges are rarely mown, so they are full of voles; a kestrel flaps into the wind at the wind's own speed and keeps its head still to watch for voles, and it can see vole wee trails that shine in ultraviolet.
fact: A peregrine falcon dives in a stoop at over 300 km/h, the fastest speed of any animal, and often nests on tall city buildings full of pigeons.
fact: Eagles see details several times more sharply than people, and an eagle's eyes are about as big as a human's.
fact: Owl wing feathers have comb-like front edges, soft fringes and a velvety surface that muffle the sound of flying.
fact: An owl can turn its head about 270 degrees, three quarters of a circle, because its eyes are fixed tubes that cannot roll; it has 14 neck bones.
fact: A barn owl's face funnels sound to ears set at different heights, so it can find a mouse in total darkness by sound alone.
fact: Owls swallow small prey whole and cough up the fur and bones as a pellet.
fact: Vultures ride thermals, columns of warm air rising from ground heated by the sun, so they soar most in the hot middle of the day, and glide for hours looking for dead animals; their strong stomach acid kills germs in rotting meat.
fact: The bald eagle has a white-feathered head; its name comes from an old word, balde, meaning white, and young bald eagles are brown all over until about five years old.
fact: An osprey dives feet first and grips slippery fish with spiky toe pads and an outer toe that swings back, two toes in front and two behind, and carries the fish head forward so it slips through the air.
fact: Harris's hawks hunt in family groups, which is rare for birds of prey, and they are often trained by falconers.
fact: The wise owl idea comes from ancient Greece, where the owl was the bird of Athena, goddess of wisdom; in puzzle tests crows and parrots do much better.
exercise: How does a kestrel hover over a grass verge? | It flaps into the wind at the wind's own speed and keeps its head still | It hangs from a thin thread of silk | It stands on the rising heat from the cars and floats on it without moving a feather | It holds its breath to become lighter
exercise: Why are motorway verges good places for kestrels? | They are full of voles because they are rarely mown | Kestrels eat the rubbish people throw | The noise of the cars scares mice out | Kestrels like to sit and watch the traffic go by all day long
exercise: What special sight helps a kestrel find voles? | It sees vole wee trails that shine in ultraviolet | It can see through the soil | It sees heat like a camera | It has a second pair of eyes on the back of its head to see behind it
exercise: How fast can a peregrine falcon dive? | Over 300 km/h, faster than any other animal | About 30 km/h, like a bicycle | About 3,000 km/h, faster than sound | About as fast as a running horse
exercise: Why do peregrines nest on cathedral towers? | Tall buildings are like cliffs, and cities have lots of pigeons | They like the sound of the bells | Towers are kept warm by the many people inside, and the eggs need heat | Priests feed them every morning
exercise: How well can a golden eagle see? | It sees details several times more sharply than a person | About as well as a person | It is nearly blind and hunts only by smell, like a dog does in the woods | It only sees black and white blobs
exercise: Why does a barn owl fly without a sound? | Fringed, soft feathers muffle the noise of its wings | It does not flap, only glides | It flies only when it rains | Its wings are made of thin skin like a bat's, not of feathers at all
exercise: How far can an owl turn its head? | About 270 degrees, three quarters of a circle | All the way round, 360 degrees, again and again | Only a little, like a person | Exactly upside down and no further
exercise: Why does an owl turn its whole head to look around? | Its eyes are fixed tubes that cannot roll | It has no neck muscles at all, so it swings it | It wants to show off | Its eyes are too heavy to move
exercise: What is the barn owl's heart-shaped face for? | It funnels sounds into the ears like a dish | It scares the mice with its pale, ghostly colour | It keeps the rain off its eyes | It shows other owls that it is happy
exercise: What is an owl pellet? | Fur and bones the owl coughs up | An owl's egg that never hatched out | Dried-up owl droppings in the barn | A ball of mud the owl builds with
exercise: How do vultures stay up for hours without flapping? | They ride thermals of rising warm air | They float like big balloons full of gas | They have very small, fast-beating wings | They never fly for more than a single minute
exercise: Why can vultures eat rotten meat without getting ill? | Their very strong stomach acid kills dangerous germs | They only eat the meat after cooking it in the hot sun on the rocks | They are never really eating it | They wash it in rivers first
exercise: Why is the bald eagle called bald? | From an old word, balde, meaning white | Because its white head has no feathers | Because it loses all its head feathers in the winter | Because it looks like a bald, wrinkly old man
exercise: How does an osprey hold a slippery fish? | Spiky toe pads, and two toes in front and two behind | It carries it in its beak only, holding it by the tail | It spears it with its tail | It wraps its wings round it
exercise: What is unusual about how Harris's hawks hunt? | They hunt in family groups | They hunt only underwater, for fish | They hunt only small insects | They never hunt, they steal from foxes
exercise: Where does the idea of the wise owl come from? | Ancient Greece, where the owl was Athena's bird | Scientists who tested owls with puzzles and found they were the cleverest birds | Owls going to school in the forest | Owls wearing glasses to read
test: In a cartoon an owl spins its head round and round like a wheel. What is true? | It can turn about three quarters of a circle, not all the way round | It can spin its head forever | It can take its head off and put it back on | It cannot turn its head at all
test: A picture book shows a wise old owl with glasses who solves every riddle. Which birds really do better at puzzles? | Crows and parrots | Pigeons and farm ducks | Chickens and geese | Owls are the best at puzzles
test: my brother reckons bald eagles r bald like grandad. are they? | No, they have white feathers on the head; balde meant white | Yes, their heads have bare skin | Yes, they lose all their feathers when they get old, like grandad's hair | Only the females are bald
test: You see a young bald eagle that is brown all over. Why no white head? | Young bald eagles get the white head at about five years old | It is a different kind of eagle that lives in the mountains | It has rolled in mud | It is ill and losing colour
test: What would happen if a barn owl's flight were as noisy as a pigeon's? | Mice would hear it coming, and it would hear them less well | It would catch more mice | Nothing, mice are deaf | It would fly faster
test: A barn owl catches a vole under snow it cannot see. How? | By sound, with ears at different heights telling it exactly where | By smell through the snow | By guessing and digging everywhere | By seeing the heat of its body through the snow, like a camera
test: Why do vultures circle over the savannah in the middle of a hot day, not at dawn? | The hot ground makes the rising air they need | Dead animals only ever appear at noon, when the sun is high | They cannot see in the morning | They are hiding from the sun
test: oi, why does my teacher make us wash hands after the owl pellet lesson? | Pellets hold old bones and fur, so washing is basic hygiene | Owl pellets are poisonous to touch, even for a moment, and burn the skin | Owls put magic in their pellets | Pellets are made of owl poo
test: A falconer's Harris's hawk lands on her glove. Why is the glove thick leather? | To protect her hand from the hawk's sharp talons | To keep the hawk warm | Because hawks like the smell of leather | To make the hawk look bigger
test: What would happen if a peregrine dived with its wings spread wide open? | The air would slow it down a lot | It would go even faster | It would fly backwards | It would land on the cathedral
test: In a game, an eagle grabs a fish from a lake with its beak while flying. Which real bird is built to grab fish, and how? | The osprey, diving feet first and gripping with its toes | The barn owl, with its beak only | The vulture, with its strong stomach and bald head | The kestrel, with its tail
test: Why is the osprey's fish turned head forward in its feet? | So it slips through the air like a torpedo | So the fish can see where they are both going on the way to the nest | So the fish does not bite the osprey | So the fish can breathe
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an owl rotates its head rather than its eyes. | The eyes are tubular and fixed within the skull | The eyes are entirely absent in the nocturnal species | The neck possesses no bones | The eyes are sensitive to rotation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the owl pellet? | It comprises the indigestible fur and bones regurgitated by the owl | It is an unfertilised egg | It is the owl's ordinary dropping | It is a structure of twigs and moss built by the owl for nesting in the spring
exam: Ascertain the maximal velocity attained by the peregrine falcon in its stoop. | In excess of three hundred kilometres per hour | Approximately thirty kilometres per hour | Approximately three thousand kilometres per hour | Approximately sixty kilometres per hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the designation «bald eagle». | It derives from an archaic word signifying white | It denotes the absence of feathers upon the head | It denotes baldness acquired in old age | It refers to the eagle's bare feet
